|~ `::span`, the kind of a stretch of source text, and what is declared
   under it: the points its kind decodes from its numbers.
   The rules it holds: [QSPRN#span]. ~|

:span
  |~~ A span is a stretch of source text, where a reading
      stopped: six whole numbers under its tag, the offset from zero, the
      line and the column from one of its start, then of the point after
      its end, `::span[0 1 1 1 1 2]` for the first character of a text.
      Its kind names each of its six numbers by its index, `startOffset`,
      `startLine`, `startColumn`, `endOffset`, `endLine` and `endColumn`,
      and its two ends, `start` and `end`, each the point it decodes from
      three of the numbers, so the line where a parse stopped is read by its
      names, `"[1" | parse !| location | startLine`.
      ::snippet~("~{" | parse !| location | legend)
      ::link~(::point | doc) ::link~(::quote/ParseError | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts |~~ The six numbers, the start and the end. ~~| [::number]
    | wires :start
        |~~ The point the span starts at: the offset, the line and the
            column of its first character, decoded from its first three
            numbers: `::span[0 1 1 4 1 5] | start` answers
            `::point[0 1 1]`.
        ~~|
        ~(take 3 | tag ::point) ::point
    | wires :end
        |~~ The point after the span's end: the offset, the line and the
            column past its last character, decoded from its last three
            numbers: `::span[0 1 1 4 1 5] | end` answers
            `::point[4 1 5]`.
        ~~|
        ~(drop 3 | tag ::point) ::point
    | wires :startOffset |~~ The offset of the start, counted from zero. ~~| /0 ::number
    | wires :startLine |~~ The line of the start, counted from one. ~~| /1 ::number
    | wires :startColumn |~~ The column of the start, counted from one. ~~| /2 ::number
    | wires :endOffset |~~ The offset past the end, counted from zero. ~~| /3 ::number
    | wires :endLine |~~ The line past the end, counted from one. ~~| /4 ::number
    | wires :endColumn |~~ The column past the end, counted from one. ~~| /5 ::number
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ A span is the vector of six numbers, the offset, the line and
            the column of its start and of the point after its end,
            `::span[0 1 1 1 1 2]`, which the location of a refusal of a
            parse holds.
        ~~|
    | throws :ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each part of a span is a whole number, an offset from zero or a
            line or a column from one.
        ~~|
    | throws :end/NotReadError
        |~~ A span holds six numbers, the point of its start and the point
            after its end, three numbers each: `:actualValue` holds the
            refusal of the point its last numbers make.
            ::link~(::point | doc)
        ~~|
    | proves :readsItsEndsAsPoints
        |~~ The ends of a span are the points of its first three numbers
            and of its last three.
        ~~|
        ~(::span[0 1 1 4 1 5] | get [~(start) ~(end)] | eq [::point[0 1 1] ::point[4 1 5]])
    | proves :namesEachNumberAtItsPlace
        |~~ Each of the six numbers of a span is read by its name. ~~|
        ~(::span[0 1 1 4 1 5] | get [~(startOffset) ~(startLine) ~(startColumn) ~(endOffset) ~(endLine) ~(endColumn)] | eq [0 1 1 4 1 5])
    | proves :readsTheLineWhereAParseStopped
        |~~ The names of the span and of its point read the line where a
            parse stopped.
        ~~|
        ~("[1\n2" | parse !| location | start | line | eq 2)
    | proves :end/NotReadError/refusesThreeNumbers
        |~~ Three numbers make a point, no span. ~~|
        ~(::span[0 1 1] !| get [~(tag) ~(found | tag)] | eq [::span/end/NotReadError ::point/offset/NotGivenError])
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesText
        |~~ Text is no vector of numbers. ~~|
        ~(::span"abcdef" !| tag | eq ::span/PayloadKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/namesTheElement
        |~~ An element that is no number is refused by its index. ~~|
        ~(::span[0 1 "x" 1 1 2] !| index | eq 2)
